   "item": {
    "subject_key": "ap-statistics",
    "unit_number": 2,
    "topic_code": "2.12",
    "topic_title": "Sampling Distributions and the Central Limit Theorem",
    "stem": "Commute times for a city's residents have a strongly right-skewed distribution. For a fixed sample size n, imagine taking every possible random sample of n residents, with replacement, and recording each sample's mean commute time. Repeat this process for increasingly large n. Which statement correctly describes what the central limit theorem predicts?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "The distribution of commute times in the city's population becomes approximately normal as n grows.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Population-versus-statistic confusion: the central limit theorem describes sample means, not individual residents' commute times. Increasing the sample size does not change the population distribution. Fix: Identify whether each plotted value represents an individual or a sample mean."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "The distribution of sample mean commute times becomes approximately normal as n grows.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "Each value in this distribution summarizes a whole sample, so this is a sampling distribution of the sample mean. The central limit theorem says its normal approximation improves as sample size grows, even though the population remains right-skewed."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "The distribution of individual commute times within each sample becomes approximately normal as n grows.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Sample-data-versus-sampling-distribution confusion: a sample's individual observations are not sample means from repeated samples. The central limit theorem does not promise that the data within a large sample will be approximately normal. Fix: Check whether the distribution collects observations within one sample or means across samples."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "The distribution of sample mean commute times is exactly normal for every sample size n.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Exact-normality trap: the central limit theorem gives an improving approximation, not exact normality for every sample size. When n = 1, the sample mean has the same right-skewed distribution as the population. Fix: Test the claim at n = 1 and distinguish approximate from exact normality."
     }
    ]
   },
